  <sect2 id="agensgraph-example-data-edges">
   <title>Connecting Them</title>

   <para>
    An <literal>acted_in</literal> edge carries the character name as an edge property.
   </para>

<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name:'Keanu Reeves'}),          (m:movie {title:'The Matrix'})                 CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Neo'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Keanu Reeves'}),          (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Reloaded'})        CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Neo'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Keanu Reeves'}),          (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Revolutions'})     CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Neo'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Keanu Reeves'}),          (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Resurrections'})   CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Neo'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Laurence Fishburne'}),    (m:movie {title:'The Matrix'})                 CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Morpheus'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Laurence Fishburne'}),    (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Reloaded'})        CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Morpheus'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Carrie-Anne Moss'}),      (m:movie {title:'The Matrix'})                 CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Trinity'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Carrie-Anne Moss'}),      (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Resurrections'})   CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Trinity'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Leonardo DiCaprio'}),     (m:movie {title:'Inception'})                  CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Dom Cobb'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Matthew McConaughey'}),   (m:movie {title:'Interstellar'})               CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Cooper'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Harrison Ford'}),         (m:movie {title:'Blade Runner'})               CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Rick Deckard'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Harrison Ford'}),         (m:movie {title:'Blade Runner 2049'})          CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Rick Deckard'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Ryan Gosling'}),          (m:movie {title:'Blade Runner 2049'})          CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'K'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Tom Cruise'}),            (m:movie {title:'Minority Report'})            CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'John Anderton'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Sam Neill'}),             (m:movie {title:'Jurassic Park'})              CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Alan Grant'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Arnold Schwarzenegger'}), (m:movie {title:'The Terminator'})             CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'The Terminator'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Arnold Schwarzenegger'}), (m:movie {title:'Terminator 2: Judgment Day'}) CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'The Terminator'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Arnold Schwarzenegger'}), (m:movie {title:'Total Recall'})               CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Douglas Quaid'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Linda Hamilton'}),        (m:movie {title:'The Terminator'})             CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Sarah Connor'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Linda Hamilton'}),        (m:movie {title:'Terminator 2: Judgment Day'}) CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Sarah Connor'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Sam Worthington'}),       (m:movie {title:'Avatar'})                     CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Jake Sully'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Matt Damon'}),            (m:movie {title:'Elysium'})                    CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Max Da Costa'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Ethan Hawke'}),           (m:movie {title:'Gattaca'})                    CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Vincent Freeman'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Bruce Willis'}),          (m:movie {title:'The Fifth Element'})          CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Korben Dallas'}]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Robert Downey Jr.'}),     (m:movie {title:'The Avengers'})               CREATE (p)-[:acted_in {role:'Tony Stark'}]->(m);
</programlisting>

   <para>
    A <literal>directed</literal> edge carries no properties.
   </para>

<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lana Wachowski'}),    (m:movie {title:'The Matrix'})                 CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lilly Wachowski'}),   (m:movie {title:'The Matrix'})                 CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lana Wachowski'}),    (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Reloaded'})        CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lilly Wachowski'}),   (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Reloaded'})        CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lana Wachowski'}),    (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Revolutions'})     CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lilly Wachowski'}),   (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Revolutions'})     CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Lana Wachowski'}),    (m:movie {title:'The Matrix Resurrections'})   CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Christopher Nolan'}), (m:movie {title:'Inception'})                  CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Christopher Nolan'}), (m:movie {title:'Interstellar'})               CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Ridley Scott'}),      (m:movie {title:'Blade Runner'})               CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Denis Villeneuve'}),  (m:movie {title:'Blade Runner 2049'})          CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Steven Spielberg'}),  (m:movie {title:'Minority Report'})            CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Steven Spielberg'}),  (m:movie {title:'Jurassic Park'})              CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'James Cameron'}),     (m:movie {title:'The Terminator'})             CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'James Cameron'}),     (m:movie {title:'Terminator 2: Judgment Day'}) CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'James Cameron'}),     (m:movie {title:'Avatar'})                     CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Paul Verhoeven'}),    (m:movie {title:'Total Recall'})               CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Neill Blomkamp'}),    (m:movie {title:'Elysium'})                    CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Andrew Niccol'}),     (m:movie {title:'Gattaca'})                    CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Luc Besson'}),        (m:movie {title:'The Fifth Element'})          CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
MATCH (p:person {name:'Joss Whedon'}),       (m:movie {title:'The Avengers'})               CREATE (p)-[:directed]->(m);
</programlisting>

   <para>
    A <literal>related_to</literal> edge carries a <literal>weight</literal>, which the
    weighted path examples use.
   </para>

<programlisting>
MATCH (a:movie {title:'The Matrix'}),             (b:movie {title:'The Matrix Reloaded'})       CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 1}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'The Matrix Reloaded'}),    (b:movie {title:'The Matrix Revolutions'})    CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 1}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'The Matrix Revolutions'}), (b:movie {title:'The Matrix Resurrections'})  CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 1}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'The Matrix'}),             (b:movie {title:'Total Recall'})              CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 4}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'The Matrix'}),             (b:movie {title:'The Terminator'})            CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 3}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'Total Recall'}),           (b:movie {title:'Inception'})                 CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 2}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'The Terminator'}),         (b:movie {title:'Terminator 2: Judgment Day'})CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 1}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'Blade Runner'}),           (b:movie {title:'Blade Runner 2049'})         CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 1}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'Interstellar'}),           (b:movie {title:'Avatar'})                    CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 5}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'Jurassic Park'}),          (b:movie {title:'The Avengers'})              CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 5}]->(b);
MATCH (a:movie {title:'Minority Report'}),        (b:movie {title:'Inception'})                 CREATE (a)-[:related_to {weight: 2}]->(b);
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-example-data-table">
   <title>Adding the Relational Table</title>

<programlisting>
CREATE TABLE rating (title text, source text, score numeric(3,1));

INSERT INTO rating VALUES
  ('The Matrix', 'critics', 8.7),                 ('The Matrix', 'audience', 9.1),
  ('The Matrix Reloaded', 'critics', 7.2),        ('The Matrix Revolutions', 'critics', 6.7),
  ('The Matrix Resurrections', 'critics', 5.7),   ('Inception', 'critics', 8.8),
  ('Inception', 'audience', 9.0),                 ('Interstellar', 'critics', 8.7),
  ('Interstellar', 'audience', 8.6),              ('Blade Runner', 'critics', 8.1),
  ('Blade Runner 2049', 'critics', 8.0),          ('Jurassic Park', 'critics', 8.2),
  ('The Terminator', 'critics', 8.0),             ('Terminator 2: Judgment Day', 'critics', 8.6),
  ('Avatar', 'critics', 7.9),                     ('Total Recall', 'critics', 7.5),
  ('Minority Report', 'critics', 7.6),            ('Gattaca', 'critics', 7.8),
  ('The Fifth Element', 'critics', 7.6),          ('Elysium', 'critics', 6.6),
  ('The Avengers', 'critics', 8.0);
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-example-data-check">
   <title>Checking What You Loaded</title>

<programlisting>
MATCH (n) RETURN label(n) AS label, count(*) AS n ORDER BY label;

  label   | n
----------+----
 "movie"  | 18
 "person" | 28
(2 rows)
</programlisting>

<programlisting>
MATCH ()-[e]->() RETURN label(e) AS label, count(*) AS n ORDER BY label;

    label     | n
--------------+----
 "acted_in"   | 25
 "directed"   | 21
 "related_to" | 11
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-example-data-tables">
   <title>The Same Data as Tables</title>

   <para>
    A graph is a schema and a label is a table, so the graph you just created is also a set of
    ordinary tables that SQL can read. <command>\dGv</command> lists the vertex labels,
    <command>\dGe</command> the edge labels, and <command>\d</command> shows the shape of one
    label.
   </para>

<programlisting>
SELECT id, properties->>'title' AS title FROM moviekb.movie ORDER BY id LIMIT 3;

 id  |         title
-----+------------------------
 3.1 | The Matrix
 3.2 | The Matrix Reloaded
 3.3 | The Matrix Revolutions
(3 rows)
</programlisting>

   <para>
    An edge table carries the two endpoint ids as well.
   </para>

<programlisting>
SELECT id, start, "end", properties FROM moviekb.acted_in ORDER BY id LIMIT 3;

 id  | start | end |   properties
-----+-------+-----+-----------------
 5.1 | 4.1   | 3.1 | {"role": "Neo"}
 5.2 | 4.1   | 3.2 | {"role": "Neo"}
 5.3 | 4.1   | 3.3 | {"role": "Neo"}
(3 rows)
</programlisting>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-example-data-drop">
   <title>Starting Over</title>

<programlisting>
DROP GRAPH moviekb CASCADE;
DROP TABLE rating;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-config">
  <title>Configuration Settings</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-config">
   <primary>configuration</primary>
   <secondary>AgensGraph parameters</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   These parameters are added by AgensGraph. Everything else in
   <xref linkend="runtime-config"/> applies unchanged. Each one can be set the usual ways: in
   <filename>postgresql.conf</filename>, on the command line, or with <command>SET</command> in a
   session, subject to the context noted in its description.
  </para>

  <variablelist>

   <varlistentry id="guc-graph-path" xreflabel="graph_path">
    <term>
     <varname>graph_path</varname> (<type>string</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>graph_path</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Sets the graph that unqualified label names belong to. It is the graph
      equivalent of <xref linkend="guc-search-path"/>. A session that has not set it cannot resolve
      an unqualified label, and a Cypher statement that names one raises an error.
     </para>
     <para>
      The default is the empty string, which means no graph is selected. Set it per session,
      or once for a database with <command>ALTER DATABASE ... SET graph_path</command>.
     </para>
     <para>
      The server reports this parameter to the client, so a driver is told the value at
      connection time and again whenever it changes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-agversion" xreflabel="agversion">
    <term>
     <varname>agversion</varname> (<type>string</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>agversion</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Shows the AgensGraph server version. This parameter is set when the
      server is built and cannot be changed.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-allow-null-properties" xreflabel="allow_null_properties">
    <term>
     <varname>allow_null_properties</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>allow_null_properties</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Controls what happens to a property whose value is null.
      When this is on the property is stored with a null value. When it is off, which is the
      default, the property is left out of the property map.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-auto-gather-graphmeta" xreflabel="auto_gather_graphmeta">
    <term>
     <varname>auto_gather_graphmeta</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>auto_gather_graphmeta</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Keeps the <structname>ag_graphmeta</structname>
      connectivity catalog current, and lets the planner use it to prune label scans. The default is
      off. Only a superuser can change it.
     </para>
     <para>
      Turning it on builds a baseline once from the edges that already exist. After that
      every edge write keeps the catalog current, including Cypher writes, SQL
      <command>INSERT</command> and <command>UPDATE</command>, <command>COPY</command> into an edge
      label, and logical replication apply.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-cypher-allow-unsafe-ddl" xreflabel="cypher_allow_unsafe_ddl">
    <term>
     <varname>cypher_allow_unsafe_ddl</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>cypher_allow_unsafe_ddl</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Skips two catalog checks that normally protect a graph
      from DDL that would leave it inconsistent. The default is off. Turn it on only to repair a
      graph, and turn it off again afterwards.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-eager-mem" xreflabel="eager_mem">
    <term>
     <varname>eager_mem</varname> (<type>integer</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>eager_mem</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Sets the maximum memory an eager plan node may use. The default is
      4MB and the minimum is 1MB. If the value is given without units it is taken as kilobytes.
     </para>
     <para>
      The limit applies to each node separately, so a plan with several eager nodes, or a
      query running in several parallel workers, can use a multiple of this value.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-enable-eager" xreflabel="enable_eager">
    <term>
     <varname>enable_eager</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>enable_eager</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Lets the planner materialize the output of a write clause before a
      later clause reads it. The default is on. Turning it off is a debugging aid and can make a
      statement that reads its own writes fail.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-enable-graph-ddl" xreflabel="enable_graph_ddl">
    <term>
     <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Allows SQL DDL to reshape a graph label. The default is off.
     </para>
     <para>
      While it is off, <command>ALTER TABLE</command> on a label, renaming a label column,
      moving a label to another schema, and a SQL <command>MERGE</command> that writes a label are
      all refused. Use <command>ALTER VLABEL</command> or <command>ALTER ELABEL</command> instead.
      Changing the type of the columns every vertex or edge is made of, which are
      <structfield>id</structfield>, <structfield>properties</structfield>,
      <structfield>start</structfield> and <structfield>end</structfield>, stays refused even when
      this is on.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-enable-graph-dml" xreflabel="enable_graph_dml">
    <term>
     <varname>enable_graph_dml</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>enable_graph_dml</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Allows SQL <command>INSERT</command>,
      <command>UPDATE</command>, <command>DELETE</command> and <command>MERGE</command> to write a
      label table directly. The default is off, which keeps writes going through Cypher, where the
      graph invariants are enforced. Only a superuser can change it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-enable-graph-endpoint-elision" xreflabel="enable_graph_endpoint_elision">
    <term>
     <varname>enable_graph_endpoint_elision</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>enable_graph_endpoint_elision</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Lets the planner remove the scan and the join for
      a pattern endpoint that the query never reads, such as the <literal>()</literal> in
      <literal>MATCH (a)-[b]-&gt;()</literal>. The default is on.
     </para>
     <para>
      The rewrite is safe because a vertex id is a primary key, so the join matches one
      row, and every edge endpoint points at a live vertex, so the join removes none. Results do not
      change. Turn it off to compare plans.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-enable-multiple-update" xreflabel="enable_multiple_update">
    <term>
     <varname>enable_multiple_update</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>enable_multiple_update</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Controls whether one statement may write the same graph
      element more than once. The default is on.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry id="guc-enable-property-promotion" xreflabel="enable_property_promotion">
    <term>
     <varname>enable_property_promotion</varname> (<type>boolean</type>)
     <indexterm>
      <primary><varname>enable_property_promotion</varname> configuration parameter</primary>
     </indexterm>
    </term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Resolves a property read such as
      <literal>n.key</literal> to a promoted typed column when the label has one. The default is
      on.
     </para>
     <para>
      Unlike the other planner switches, turning this one off can change an answer, not
      only a plan. A resolved read compares and orders using the rules of the column type. The
      fallback reads the property map and uses the rules of <type>jsonb</type>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-catalogs">
  <title>System Catalogs and Views</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-catalogs">
   <primary>system catalog</primary>
   <secondary>AgensGraph</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   AgensGraph adds four catalogs to <literal>pg_catalog</literal> and two views. Everything in
   <xref linkend="catalogs"/> applies unchanged. A graph is a schema and a label is a table, so a
   graph also appears in <structname>pg_namespace</structname> and a label in
   <structname>pg_class</structname>. The catalogs here record what makes them a graph.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="catalog-ag-graph">
   <title><structname>ag_graph</structname></title>

   <indexterm zone="catalog-ag-graph"><primary>ag_graph</primary></indexterm>

   <para>One row for each graph.</para>

  <table id="catalog-ag-graph-table">
   <title><structname>ag_graph</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Column</entry>
       <entry>Type</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>oid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>Row identifier.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>graphname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the graph, which is also the name of its schema.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>nspid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>The schema that holds the graph, in <structname>pg_namespace</structname>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>graphmeta_valid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>bool</type></entry>
       <entry>True when this graph has a complete <structname>ag_graphmeta</structname> baseline that has been kept current since it was gathered, which is what lets the planner prune scans against it. <function>regather_graphmeta()</function> sets it. It is cleared when an edge write commits while <xref linkend="guc-auto-gather-graphmeta"/> is off, because that write changed connectivity without recording it. A new empty graph is valid.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="catalog-ag-label">
   <title><structname>ag_label</structname></title>

   <indexterm zone="catalog-ag-label"><primary>ag_label</primary></indexterm>

   <para>One row for each vertex label and each edge label.</para>

  <table id="catalog-ag-label-table">
   <title><structname>ag_label</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Column</entry>
       <entry>Type</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>oid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>Row identifier.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>labname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the label.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>graphid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>The graph the label belongs to, in <structname>ag_graph</structname>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>labid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int4</type></entry>
       <entry>Identifier of the label within its graph. This value is carried in the high bits of every <type>graphid</type> the label issues, so a label is a contiguous range of graph ids.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>relid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>The table that stores the label, in <structname>pg_class</structname>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>labkind</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>char</type></entry>
       <entry><literal>v</literal> for a vertex label, <literal>e</literal> for an edge label.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="catalog-ag-label-property">
   <title><structname>ag_label_property</structname></title>

   <indexterm zone="catalog-ag-label-property"><primary>ag_label_property</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    One row for each property that a label mirrors into a typed column. This is the catalog that
    makes a promoted property resolvable, because it maps the Cypher key to the column that holds
    it. A label with no promoted property has no row here.
   </para>

  <table id="catalog-ag-label-property-table">
   <title><structname>ag_label_property</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Column</entry>
       <entry>Type</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>laboid</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>The label that owns the property, in <structname>ag_label</structname>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>propname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>The Cypher property key, down cased. It comes from the generation expression of the column rather than from the column name, so a query keeps naming the property.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>attnum</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int2</type></entry>
       <entry>Attribute number of the typed column in the label table.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>semantics</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>char</type></entry>
       <entry>Comparison semantics recorded for the property.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>

   <para>
    The pair <literal>(laboid, propname)</literal> is unique, so one key resolves to at most one
    column in a label.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="catalog-ag-graphmeta">
   <title><structname>ag_graphmeta</structname></title>

   <indexterm zone="catalog-ag-graphmeta"><primary>ag_graphmeta</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    One row for each combination of start label, edge label and end label that actually occurs in a
    graph. That is the graph's real connectivity, and the planner uses it to scan an unlabelled
    pattern element only over the labels it can match. The catalog is maintained when
    <xref linkend="guc-auto-gather-graphmeta"/> is on, and rebuilt by
    <function>regather_graphmeta()</function>.
   </para>

  <table id="catalog-ag-graphmeta-table">
   <title><structname>ag_graphmeta</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Column</entry>
       <entry>Type</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>graph</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>oid</type></entry>
       <entry>The graph, in <structname>ag_graph</structname>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>edge</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int2</type></entry>
       <entry>Label id of the edge label.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>start</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int2</type></entry>
       <entry>Label id of the label the edge starts at.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>end</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int2</type></entry>
       <entry>Label id of the label the edge ends at.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>edgecount</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int8</type></entry>
       <entry>Number of edges recorded for that combination.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="view-ag-property-indexes">
   <title><structname>ag_property_indexes</structname></title>

   <indexterm zone="view-ag-property-indexes"><primary>ag_property_indexes</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    Lists the property indexes of every label in a readable form. It shows both kinds, an
    expression index over the property map and a plain column index that binds a promoted typed
    column. This is what <command>\dGi</command> reads.
   </para>

  <table id="view-ag-property-indexes-table">
   <title><structname>ag_property_indexes</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Column</entry>
       <entry>Type</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>graphname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Graph that holds the label.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>labelname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Label the index is on.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>indexname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the index.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>tablespace</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Tablespace holding the index, when it is not the default.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>unique</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>bool</type></entry>
       <entry>True when the index is unique.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>indexdef</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>text</type></entry>
       <entry>The command that would recreate the index.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>owner</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Owner of the label.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>size</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>text</type></entry>
       <entry>Size of the index on disk.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>description</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>text</type></entry>
       <entry>Comment on the index.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="view-ag-graphmeta-view">
   <title><structname>ag_graphmeta_view</structname></title>

   <indexterm zone="view-ag-graphmeta-view"><primary>ag_graphmeta_view</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    The same rows as <structname>ag_graphmeta</structname>, with the graph and the three label ids
    resolved to names.
   </para>

  <table id="view-ag-graphmeta-view-table">
   <title><structname>ag_graphmeta_view</structname> Columns</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Column</entry>
       <entry>Type</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>graphname</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the graph.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>start</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the label the edge starts at.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>edge</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the edge label.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>end</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>name</type></entry>
       <entry>Name of the label the edge ends at.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><structfield>edgecount</structfield></entry>
       <entry><type>int8</type></entry>
       <entry>Number of edges recorded for that combination.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-datatypes">
  <title>Data Types</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-datatypes">
   <primary>data type</primary>
   <secondary>AgensGraph</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   AgensGraph adds the types below. A property value is a <type>jsonb</type>, so everything in
   <xref linkend="datatype-json"/> applies to properties.
  </para>

  <table id="agensgraph-datatypes-table">
   <title>AgensGraph Data Types</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <thead>
     <row>
       <entry>Name</entry>
       <entry>Storage Size</entry>
       <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><type>graphid</type></entry>
       <entry>8 bytes</entry>
       <entry>Identity of a vertex or an edge.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><type>vertex</type></entry>
       <entry>variable</entry>
       <entry>A vertex, with its identity and its properties.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><type>edge</type></entry>
       <entry>variable</entry>
       <entry>An edge, with its identity, its two endpoints and its properties.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><type>graphpath</type></entry>
       <entry>variable</entry>
       <entry>A path, as the vertices and the edges along it.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><type>rowid</type></entry>
       <entry>10 bytes</entry>
       <entry>Location of a row. Used internally.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>

  <sect2 id="datatype-graphid">
   <title><type>graphid</type></title>

   <indexterm zone="datatype-graphid"><primary>graphid</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    A <type>graphid</type> identifies one vertex or one edge. It is eight bytes. The high 16 bits
    are the label id, which is <structfield>labid</structfield> in
    <structname>ag_label</structname>, and the low 48 bits are the position within that label. It
    prints as the two parts joined by a dot, so <literal>3.1</literal> is the first row of the
    label whose id is 3.
   </para>

   <para>
    Because the label is part of the value, a graph id says which label a vertex belongs to without
    reading any table, and one label occupies a contiguous range of graph ids. A graph may hold up
    to 65535 labels, and a label up to 281474976710655 rows.
   </para>

   <para>
    <function>id()</function> returns the graph id of a vertex or an edge.
    <function>graphid_labid()</function> and <function>graphid_locid()</function> return the two
    parts.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="datatype-vertex-edge">
   <title><type>vertex</type> and <type>edge</type></title>

   <indexterm zone="datatype-vertex-edge"><primary>vertex</primary></indexterm>
   <indexterm zone="datatype-vertex-edge"><primary>edge</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    A <type>vertex</type> carries its graph id and its property map. An <type>edge</type> carries
    its graph id, the graph ids of the vertices it starts and ends at, and its property map.
    <function>properties()</function> returns the map, <function>label()</function> the label name,
    and <function>start_id()</function> and <function>end_id()</function> the endpoints of an edge.
   </para>

   <para>
    Both compare, sort and group by identity, which is what <literal>=</literal> has always meant
    for them. Equal properties do not make two vertices equal.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="datatype-graphpath">
   <title><type>graphpath</type></title>

   <indexterm zone="datatype-graphpath"><primary>graphpath</primary></indexterm>

   <para>
    A <type>graphpath</type> holds the vertices and the edges of a path, in order. A path of
    <replaceable>n</replaceable> edges has <replaceable>n</replaceable> plus one vertices.
    <function>nodes()</function> and <function>relationships()</function> return the two arrays,
    and <function>length()</function> returns the number of edges.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-psql">
  <title>psql Support</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-psql">
   <primary>psql</primary>
   <secondary>graph meta commands</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   <application>psql</application> gains five meta commands for graphs. They follow the conventions
   of the rest of the <command>\d</command> family described in <xref linkend="app-psql"/>. Append
   <literal>+</literal> for more detail, and give a pattern to restrict what is listed. The
   <literal>G</literal> is upper case, because <command>\dg</command> already lists roles.
  </para>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><command>\dG[+] [ <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ]</command></term>
    <listitem><para>Lists graphs.</para></listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><command>\dGl[+] [ <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ]</command></term>
    <listitem><para>Lists labels of both kinds.</para></listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><command>\dGv[+] [ <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ]</command></term>
    <listitem><para>Lists vertex labels.</para></listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><command>\dGe[+] [ <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ]</command></term>
    <listitem><para>Lists edge labels.</para></listitem>
   </varlistentry>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><command>\dGi[+] [ <replaceable class="parameter">pattern</replaceable> ]</command></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Lists property indexes, reading <structname>ag_property_indexes</structname>. Both kinds are
      shown, an expression index over the property map and a plain index that binds a promoted
      typed column.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>

  <para>
   For <command>\dGl</command>, <command>\dGv</command> and <command>\dGe</command> the second
   part of a pattern names a label. For <command>\dGi</command> it names an index. A label is an
   ordinary table, so <command>\d</command> on it shows its columns and its indexes.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-functions">
  <title>Functions</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-functions">
   <primary>function</primary>
   <secondary>AgensGraph</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   The functions below are added by AgensGraph and are callable from Cypher. Everything in
   <xref linkend="functions"/> is callable too, and a Cypher expression may use any SQL function.
   A function that takes or returns <type>jsonb</type> is the Cypher facing form, because a
   property is a <type>jsonb</type> value. Several names carry more than one signature, and where
   two entries share a name they are two different functions. <function>length</function> over a
   string is not <function>length</function> over a path, and the signature tells them apart.
  </para>

  <para>
   Aggregates are marked in their description. The three shortest path functions are usable only
   inside a <xref linkend="cypher-match"/> pattern, not as ordinary expressions.
  </para>

  <table id="agensgraph-functions-table">
   <title>AgensGraph Functions</title>
   <tgroup cols="3">
    <colspec colname="c1" colwidth="1*"/>
    <colspec colname="c2" colwidth="2*"/>
    <colspec colname="c3" colwidth="2*"/>
    <thead>
     <row>
      <entry>Function</entry>
      <entry>Signature</entry>
      <entry>Description</entry>
     </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><function>abs</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>abs ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>abs ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the absolute value of a number.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>acos</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>acos ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>acos ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the arc cosine of <literal>x</literal>, in radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>ag_get_graphconstraintdef</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>ag_get_graphconstraintdef ( constraint oid ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>Reconstructs the <literal>CREATE CONSTRAINT</literal> command for a graph constraint, given its OID.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>ag_get_propindexdef</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>ag_get_propindexdef ( index oid ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>Reconstructs the <literal>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX</literal> command for a property index, given its OID.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>ag_property_text</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>ag_property_text ( properties jsonb, key text, expected_kind "char" ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns a property's value as <literal>text</literal>, but only when the stored value is of the JSON kind asked for. New in 2.18.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>agversion</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>agversion ( ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>The AgensGraph version of the running server.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>all</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>all ( variable IN list WHERE predicate ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns true when the predicate holds for every element of the list.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>allshortestpaths</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>allshortestpaths ( pattern ) -&gt; graphpath</literal></entry>
       <entry>Every shortest path between two vertices.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>any</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>any ( variable IN list WHERE predicate ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns true when the predicate holds for at least one element of the list.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>array_size</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>array_size ( list jsonb ) -&gt; integer</literal></member><member><literal>array_size ( list json ) -&gt; integer</literal></member><member><literal>array_size ( list anyarray ) -&gt; integer</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the number of elements in an array.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>asin</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>asin ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>asin ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the arc sine of <literal>x</literal>, in radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>atan</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>atan ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>atan ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the arc tangent of <literal>x</literal>, in radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>atan2</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>atan2 ( y jsonb, x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>atan2 ( y double precision, x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Arc tangent of <literal>y / x</literal> in radians, using both signs to fix the quadrant.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>avg</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>avg ( expression numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the arithmetic mean of the non null values in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>cardinality</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>cardinality ( value jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>cardinality ( p graphpath ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the number of elements in a list, entries in a map, or vertices and edges in a path. New in 2.18.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>ceil</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>ceil ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>ceil ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the smallest integer that is not less than <literal>x</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>coalesce</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>coalesce ( value [, value ] ... ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the first argument that is not null.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>collect</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>collect ( expression anyelement ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>collect ( v vertex ) -&gt; vertex[]</literal></member><member><literal>collect ( e edge ) -&gt; edge[]</literal></member><member><literal>collect ( p graphpath ) -&gt; graphpath[]</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Gathers the group's values into a list; a graph element gives a native array.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>cos</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>cos ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>cos ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the cosine of an angle given in radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>cosd</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>cosd ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the cosine of an angle given in degrees.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>cot</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>cot ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>cot ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the cotangent of an angle given in radians, the reciprocal of the tangent.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>count</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>count ( * ) -&gt; bigint</literal></member><member><literal>count ( expression ) -&gt; bigint</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Counts rows in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>cypher_to_jsonb</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>cypher_to_jsonb ( value anyelement ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Boxes a value to <literal>jsonb</literal> using Cypher's rules; refuses a graph type.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>datetime</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>datetime ( ) -&gt; timestamp with time zone</literal></member><member><literal>datetime ( timezone "any" ) -&gt; timestamp without time zone</literal></member><member><literal>datetime ( year integer, month integer, mday integer, hour integer, min integer, sec integer, millisec integer, nanosec integer, timezone "any" ) -&gt; timestamp without time zone</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>The current transaction timestamp, the time in a named zone, or a timestamp built from parts.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>degrees</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>degrees ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>degrees ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Converts radians to degrees.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>dijkstra</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>dijkstra ( pattern, weight ) -&gt; graphpath</literal></member><member><literal>dijkstra ( pattern, weight, condition ) -&gt; graphpath</literal></member><member><literal>dijkstra ( pattern, weight, LIMIT count ) -&gt; graphpath</literal></member><member><literal>dijkstra ( pattern, weight, condition, LIMIT count ) -&gt; graphpath</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Finds the cheapest path between two vertices, where each edge costs what <literal>weight</literal> evaluates to.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>e</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>e ( ) -&gt; numeric</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns Euler's number as a <literal>numeric</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>edge_to_jsonb</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>edge_to_jsonb ( e edge ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns an edge's property bag.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>edges</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>edges ( p graphpath ) -&gt; edge[]</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the edges of a path, in order.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>end_id</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>end_id ( e edge ) -&gt; graphid</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the <literal>graphid</literal> of the vertex an edge points at, without reading that vertex.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>end_vertex</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>end_vertex ( e edge ) -&gt; vertex</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the vertex an edge points at.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>endNode</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>endNode ( e edge ) -&gt; vertex</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the vertex an edge points at.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>exists</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>exists ( pattern ) -&gt; boolean</literal></member><member><literal>exists ( property_reference ) -&gt; boolean</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>True when a pattern matches at least once, or when a property is present and not null.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>exp</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>exp ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>exp ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns *e* raised to the power <literal>x</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>floor</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>floor ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>floor ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the largest integer that is not greater than <literal>x</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>get_last_graph_write_stats</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>get_last_graph_write_stats ( ) -&gt; record</literal></entry>
       <entry>What the most recent graph write in this session changed, as five counters.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>get_time</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>get_time ( ) -&gt; time without time zone</literal></member><member><literal>get_time ( timezone "any" ) -&gt; time without time zone</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the current time of day, either locally or in a named zone.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>graph_labid</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>graph_labid ( qualified_label cstring ) -&gt; integer</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the label id of a label named as <literal>graph.label</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>graphid</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>graphid ( labid integer, locid bigint ) -&gt; graphid</literal></member><member><literal>graphid ( id numeric ) -&gt; graphid</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Builds a <literal>graphid</literal> from a label id and a local id, or from its printed form.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>graphid_in_jsonb_array</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>graphid_in_jsonb_array ( id graphid, elements jsonb ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns true when a <literal>graphid</literal> is one of the graph elements in a <literal>jsonb</literal> array.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>graphid_labid</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>graphid_labid ( id graphid ) -&gt; integer</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the label part of a <literal>graphid</literal>, the number before the dot.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>graphid_locid</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>graphid_locid ( id graphid ) -&gt; bigint</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the local part of a <literal>graphid</literal>, the number after the dot, taken from the label's sequence.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>head</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>head ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>head ( list anyarray ) -&gt; anyelement</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the first element of a list.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>id</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>id ( v vertex ) -&gt; graphid</literal></member><member><literal>id ( e edge ) -&gt; graphid</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns a graph element's identity.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>isEmpty</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>isEmpty ( value jsonb ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns true when a list has no elements or a map has no keys.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_add</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_add ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick <literal>+</literal> operator: addition, or concatenation of two strings or two lists.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_array_length</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_array_length ( list jsonb ) -&gt; integer</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the number of elements in a <literal>jsonb</literal> array, as an <literal>integer</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_div</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_div ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick <literal>/</literal> operator. Integer division when both operands are integers.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_mod</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_mod ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick <literal>%</literal> operator.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_mul</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_mul ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick <literal>*</literal> operator.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_pow</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_pow ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick <literal>^</literal> operator.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_sub</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_sub ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick binary <literal>-</literal> operator.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_uminus</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_uminus ( a jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick prefix <literal>-</literal> operator.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>jsonb_uplus</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>jsonb_uplus ( a jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements the backtick prefix <literal>+</literal> operator.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>keys</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>keys ( map jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns a list of the key names of a map, or of a graph element's properties.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>label</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>label ( v vertex ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>label ( e edge ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the name of the label a graph element was created with, as a <literal>jsonb</literal> string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>labels</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>labels ( v vertex ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns a list holding the vertex's own label and every label it inherits from, nearest first.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>last</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>last ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>last ( list anyarray ) -&gt; anyelement</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the final element of a list.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>left</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>left ( string jsonb, n jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>left ( string text, n integer ) -&gt; text</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the first <literal>n</literal> characters of a string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>length</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>length ( p graphpath ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>length ( vs vertex[] ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>length ( es edge[] ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>length ( ps graphpath[] ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Edges in a path, or elements of an array of graph elements.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>length</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>length ( value jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Characters in a string, elements in a list, or keys in a map.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>ln</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>ln ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member><member><literal>ln ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Natural logarithm of <literal>x</literal>. The function <literal>log</literal> resolves to in Cypher.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>localdatetime</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>localdatetime ( ) -&gt; timestamp with time zone</literal></member><member><literal>localdatetime ( timezone "any" ) -&gt; timestamp without time zone</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the current transaction timestamp, or the current wall clock reading in a named zone.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>log</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>log ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Natural logarithm of <literal>x</literal>, not base 10, and with no base argument.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>log10</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>log10 ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member><member><literal>log10 ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the base-10 logarithm of <literal>x</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>lower</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>lower ( string text ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the string in lower case.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>lTrim</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>lTrim ( string jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Removes whitespace from the start of a string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>max</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>max ( expression jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>max ( expression numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the largest value in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>min</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>min ( expression jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>min ( expression numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the smallest value in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>nodes</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>nodes ( p graphpath ) -&gt; vertex[]</literal></entry>
       <entry>The vertices of a path, in order.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>none</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>none ( variable IN list WHERE predicate ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns true when the predicate holds for no element of the list, the negation of <literal>any</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>percentileCont</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>percentileCont ( list jsonb, percentile numeric ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Percentile of a list of numbers, interpolated between neighbouring values.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>percentileDisc</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>percentileDisc ( list jsonb, percentile numeric ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Percentile of a list of numbers, rounded down to a value in the list.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>pi</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>pi ( ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns π.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>properties</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>properties ( v vertex ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>properties ( e edge ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns a graph element's whole property bag as a map.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>radians</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>radians ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>radians ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Converts degrees to radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>rand</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>rand ( ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns a number in the half open range 0 to 1. It is immutable, so the planner folds it to a single constant and every row of a query sees the same value. For a value that varies per row use PostgreSQL's <function>random()</function>, which is volatile and works in Cypher.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>range</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>range ( start integer, stop integer ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>range ( start integer, stop integer, step integer ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns a list of integers from <literal>start</literal> to <literal>stop</literal>, inclusive at both ends.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>regather_graphmeta</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>regather_graphmeta ( ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Empties <literal>ag_graphmeta</literal> and rebuilds it by scanning every edge label in the current graph.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>relationships</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>relationships ( p graphpath ) -&gt; edge[]</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the edges of a path, in order.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>replace</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>replace ( string jsonb, old jsonb, new jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Replaces every occurrence of one substring with another.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>reverse</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>reverse ( string text ) -&gt; text</literal></member><member><literal>reverse ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>reverse ( list anyarray ) -&gt; anyarray</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Reverses a list, or a string given as a literal or cast to <literal>text</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>right</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>right ( string jsonb, n jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>right ( string text, n integer ) -&gt; text</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the last <literal>n</literal> characters of a string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>round</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>round ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>round ( x numeric [, scale integer ] ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Rounds <literal>x</literal> to the nearest integer, halves away from zero.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>rTrim</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>rTrim ( string jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Removes whitespace from the end of a string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>shortestpath</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>shortestpath ( pattern ) -&gt; graphpath</literal></entry>
       <entry>Finds one shortest path between two vertices, by number of edges.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>sign</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>sign ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>sign ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns −1, 0 or 1 according to the sign of <literal>x</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>sin</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>sin ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the sine of an angle given in radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>sind</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>sind ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the sine of an angle given in degrees.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>single</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>single ( variable IN list WHERE predicate ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns true when the predicate holds for exactly one element of the list.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>size</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>size ( value ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>size ( pattern ) -&gt; bigint</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>The length of a value, or the number of times a pattern matches.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>split</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>split ( string text, delimiter text ) -&gt; anyarray</literal></entry>
       <entry>Cuts a string into a list at every occurrence of a delimiter.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>sqrt</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>sqrt ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>sqrt ( x numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the square root of <literal>x</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>start_id</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>start_id ( e edge ) -&gt; graphid</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the <literal>graphid</literal> of the vertex an edge leaves, without reading that vertex.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>start_vertex</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>start_vertex ( e edge ) -&gt; vertex</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the vertex an edge leaves.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>startNode</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>startNode ( e edge ) -&gt; vertex</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the vertex an edge leaves.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>stdev</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>stdev ( expression numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></entry>
       <entry>Sample standard deviation of the values in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>stdevp</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>stdevp ( expression numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></entry>
       <entry>Population standard deviation of the values in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>str_size</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>str_size ( string text ) -&gt; integer</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the number of characters in a string, as an <literal>integer</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>string_contains</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>string_contains ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements <literal>CONTAINS</literal>. True when the right string occurs anywhere in the left.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>string_ends_with</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>string_ends_with ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements <literal>ENDS WITH</literal>. True when the left string ends with the right.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>string_regex</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>string_regex ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements <literal>=~</literal>. True when the left string matches the right as a regular expression.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>string_starts_with</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>string_starts_with ( a jsonb, b jsonb ) -&gt; boolean</literal></entry>
       <entry>Implements <literal>STARTS WITH</literal>. True when the left string begins with the right.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>substring</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>substring ( string jsonb, start jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>substring ( string jsonb, start jsonb, count jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns part of a string, starting at <literal>start</literal> and running to the end or for <literal>count</literal> characters.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>sum</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>sum ( expression numeric ) -&gt; numeric</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the total of the non null values in the group.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>tail</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>tail ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>tail ( list anyarray ) -&gt; anyarray</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns every element of a list except the first.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>tan</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>tan ( x jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>tan ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the tangent of an angle given in radians.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>tand</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>tand ( x double precision ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the tangent of an angle given in degrees.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>to_jsonb</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>to_jsonb ( value anyelement ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Converts any SQL value to <literal>jsonb</literal>, a composite becoming an object.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toBoolean</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>toBoolean ( value text ) -&gt; boolean</literal></member><member><literal>toBoolean ( value "any" ) -&gt; boolean</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the boolean a value names, or null when it names none.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toBooleanOrNull</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>toBooleanOrNull ( value text ) -&gt; boolean</literal></member><member><literal>toBooleanOrNull ( value "any" ) -&gt; boolean</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Returns the boolean a value names, or null.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toFloat</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>toFloat ( value "any" ) -&gt; double precision</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the floating point number a value names, or null. New in 2.18.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toFloatList</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>toFloatList ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>toFloatList ( list anyarray ) -&gt; double precision[]</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Converts every element of a list to a floating point number.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toInteger</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>toInteger ( value "any" ) -&gt; bigint</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the integer a value names, or null. New in 2.18.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toIntegerList</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>toIntegerList ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>toIntegerList ( list anyarray ) -&gt; bigint[]</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Converts every element of a list to an integer.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toLower</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>toLower ( string jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the string in lower case.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toString</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>toString ( value jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the string form of a value.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toStringList</function></entry>
       <entry><simplelist type="vert" columns="1"><member><literal>toStringList ( list jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></member><member><literal>toStringList ( list anyarray ) -&gt; text[]</literal></member></simplelist></entry>
       <entry>Converts every element of a list to a string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toStringOrNull</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>toStringOrNull ( value "any" ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the string form of a value, or null when the value has no string form.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>toUpper</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>toUpper ( string jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the string in upper case.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>trim</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>trim ( string jsonb ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Removes whitespace from both ends of a string.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>type</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>type ( e edge ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the name of an edge's label, as a <literal>jsonb</literal> string, the same value as <literal>label(e)</literal>, under the name Cypher uses for relationship types.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>unnest</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>unnest ( from_json jsonb ) -&gt; setof jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Expands a list into one row per element. New in 2.18.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>upper</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>upper ( string text ) -&gt; text</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the string in upper case.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>vertex_to_jsonb</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>vertex_to_jsonb ( v vertex ) -&gt; jsonb</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns a vertex's property bag.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><function>vertices</function></entry>
       <entry><literal>vertices ( p graphpath ) -&gt; vertex[]</literal></entry>
       <entry>Returns the vertices of a path, in order.</entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
   </tgroup>
  </table>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-upgrading">
  <title>Upgrading from 2.17</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-upgrading">
   <primary>upgrading</primary>
   <secondary>AgensGraph 2.18</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   AgensGraph 2.18 is built on PostgreSQL 18, so moving to it is a major version upgrade. The
   data directory format changed and a 2.17 data directory cannot be started by 2.18 binaries.
   Use <xref linkend="pgupgrade"/> or a dump and reload, exactly as for any PostgreSQL major
   version. What follows is only the part that is specific to AgensGraph.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-upgrading-breaking">
   <title>Changes That Break Working Queries</title>

   <para>
    Each item below turns 2.17 code into an error or a different answer. None of them is caught
    by the upgrade tooling, so check an application against this list before upgrading.
   </para>

   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>New reserved words.</emphasis> Six words became reserved:
      <literal>insert</literal>, <literal>finish</literal>, <literal>let</literal>,
      <literal>xor</literal>, <literal>current_graph</literal> and
      <literal>current_property_graph</literal>. Which positions break is not uniform, so
      check a name against <xref linkend="agensgraph-upgrading-reserved-table"/>.
      <command>CREATE VLABEL</command> and <command>CREATE ELABEL</command> refuse all six
      unquoted. Rename, or quote with double quotes. A backtick is not a quoting character.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>A condition takes only a boolean.</emphasis> <literal>WHERE n.count</literal>
      and <literal>FILTER n.name</literal> used to select on a value that was not zero and not
      empty. They now raise a cast error. Compare explicitly, or add
      <literal>::boolean</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>A collected element is a value, not a picture.</emphasis>
      <function>collect()</function> over a vertex, an edge or a path returns a native array.
      Client code that parsed the old <type>jsonb</type> form has to change. Code that reads
      properties off the collected elements starts working.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis><function>min()</function> and <function>max()</function> over a vertex or an
      edge</emphasis> are now ambiguous and raise an error. Write
      <literal>min(v::jsonb)</literal> or <literal>min(id(v))</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Changed return types.</emphasis> <function>range()</function> returns a
      <type>jsonb</type> array. <function>toStringList</function>,
      <function>toIntegerList</function> and <function>toFloatList</function> return typed
      arrays. The earlier versions gave wrong answers without saying so.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>The <literal>WHERE</literal> of a <command>WITH</command> moved.</emphasis> It is
      now written before <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command> and
      <command>LIMIT</command>, and it applies before them. The 2.17 spelling, with
      <literal>WHERE</literal> last, no longer parses. Rewrite
      <literal>WITH m ORDER BY x LIMIT n WHERE cond</literal> as
      <literal>WITH m WHERE cond ORDER BY x LIMIT n</literal>. The result set changes as well as
      the spelling, because the filter now runs before the paging.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Row level security is enforced on graph writes.</emphasis> Re-test every policy
      on a label. Writes that used to slip past an <literal>INSERT</literal> policy, a
      <literal>USING</literal> clause, or an inherited label policy are now refused.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>A <command>WITH</command> may not carry one name twice.</emphasis>
      <literal>WITH a AS t, b AS t</literal> was accepted and is now refused with
      <literal>WITH passes on "t" more than once</literal>. <command>RETURN</command> still
      allows it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>A write clause runs to completion.</emphasis> A <command>LIMIT</command> after a
      write no longer cuts the number of rows the write processes.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>Label structure is protected.</emphasis> <command>ALTER TABLE</command> against
      a label is refused for anything structural. Use the label DDL, or set
      <xref linkend="guc-enable-graph-ddl"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis><literal>call</literal> before a parenthesis or a brace.</emphasis> The lexer now
      reads <literal>call</literal> followed by <literal>(</literal> or <literal>{</literal> as
      the start of a <command>CALL</command> clause. A function named <literal>call</literal> is
      then unreachable <emphasis>from SQL as well as from Cypher</emphasis>, and even
      <command>CREATE FUNCTION call(...)</command> is a syntax error. An unlabelled node pattern
      <literal>(call { ... })</literal> fails too. Double quote the name in every position:
      <literal>"call"(21)</literal> works from SQL and from Cypher, and
      <literal>("call" { ... })</literal> works as a pattern.
      <literal>MATCH (call:movie)</literal> is unaffected, because a label follows the name.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis><command>EXPLAIN</command> text changed</emphasis> for
      <function>count()</function> over an element, for property reads, and for variable length
      traversal nodes, which now name the edge label. Plan baselines need refreshing.
     </para>
    </listitem>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <emphasis>A foreign key whose two sides are collated differently is refused</emphasis>
      when either collation is nondeterministic, with
      <literal>SQLSTATE 42P21</literal>. 2.17 built it. Both sides carrying the
      <emphasis>same</emphasis> nondeterministic collation is still accepted, and so are two
      different deterministic ones. This reaches a foreign key on a label column as well.
      <literal>NOT VALID</literal> does not defer it.
     </para>
     <para>
      A dump taken from 2.17 therefore restores without the constraint. Find the pairs before
      upgrading, on the 2.17 cluster, in each database:
<programlisting>
SELECT c.conrelid::regclass AS referencing, c.conname, fa.attname AS col,
       fc.collname AS referencing_collation, pc.collname AS referenced_collation
  FROM pg_constraint c
  CROSS JOIN LATERAL unnest(c.conkey, c.confkey) AS k(fkatt, pkatt)
  JOIN pg_attribute fa ON fa.attrelid = c.conrelid AND fa.attnum = k.fkatt
  JOIN pg_attribute pa ON pa.attrelid = c.confrelid AND pa.attnum = k.pkatt
  JOIN pg_collation fc ON fc.oid = fa.attcollation
  JOIN pg_collation pc ON pc.oid = pa.attcollation
 WHERE c.contype = 'f'
   AND fa.attcollation &lt;&gt; pa.attcollation
   AND NOT (fc.collisdeterministic AND pc.collisdeterministic);
</programlisting>
      Give both sides the same collation before dumping. <xref linkend="pgupgrade"/> does not
      report these: <option>--check</option> ends with <literal>Clusters are compatible</literal>
      and the upgrade then fails when it rebuilds the constraint.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   <table id="agensgraph-upgrading-reserved-table">
    <title>Where Each Newly Reserved Word Is Refused</title>
    <tgroup cols="6">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Word</entry>
       <entry>Label in a pattern</entry>
       <entry><command>CREATE VLABEL</command></entry>
       <entry>Variable</entry>
       <entry><literal>AS</literal> alias</entry>
       <entry>Sorting by that alias</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>insert</literal></entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>finish</literal></entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>let</literal></entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>xor</literal></entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>current_graph</literal></entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>current_property_graph</literal></entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>fails</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
       <entry>works</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>

   <para>
    Two more differences are worth knowing, although neither can break 2.17 code because
    neither construct parsed on 2.17. A graph element may now be tested for membership of a
    list, and the test compares identity, so two vertices with equal properties are not equal.
    And a property that has been promoted to a typed column reads back in that column's type
    rather than as <type>jsonb</type>, which a driver expecting JSON for every Cypher column
    will notice as soon as the feature is adopted.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-upgrading-checksums">
   <title>Data Checksums</title>

   <para>
    PostgreSQL 18 turns data page checksums on by default and PostgreSQL 17 had them off, so
    the new cluster has to be initialized to match the old one or
    <application>pg_upgrade</application> stops with <literal>old cluster does not use data
    checksums but the new one does</literal>. Read the old cluster's setting first:
   </para>

<programlisting>
pg_controldata -D <replaceable>olddatadir</replaceable> | grep checksum
</programlisting>

   <para>
    If it reports <literal>Data page checksum version: 0</literal>, pass
    <option>--no-data-checksums</option> to <application>initdb</application> for the new
    cluster. If it reports <literal>1</literal>, leave the option off. Turning checksums on is a
    separate operation, done afterwards with <xref linkend="app-pgchecksums"/>.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-upgrading-after">
   <title>After the Upgrade</title>

   <para>
    Three tasks are specific to AgensGraph and none of them is optional.
   </para>

   <para>
    <emphasis>Rebuild statistics.</emphasis> <application>pg_upgrade</application> does not
    carry optimizer statistics across, so plans stay poor until they are rebuilt with
    <xref linkend="app-vacuumdb"/> and <option>--analyze-in-stages</option>.
   </para>

   <para>
    <emphasis>Re-gather graph metadata.</emphasis> <structname>ag_graphmeta</structname> drives
    the planner's scan pruning. A binary upgrade leaves it empty and marks the graph invalid, so
    pruning stays off until it is rebuilt, once for each graph:
   </para>

<programlisting>
SET graph_path = <replaceable>graph</replaceable>;
SELECT regather_graphmeta();

SELECT graphname, graphmeta_valid FROM pg_catalog.ag_graph;
</programlisting>

   <para>
    <emphasis>Re-tune edge label statistics.</emphasis> 2.18 lowered the statistics target on a
    new edge label's <structfield>start</structfield> and <structfield>end</structfield> columns
    from 10000 to 1000. A label created on 2.17 keeps the old value and pays a large planning
    cost on every edge join. For each edge label:
   </para>

<programlisting>
ALTER TABLE <replaceable>graph</replaceable>.<replaceable>elabel</replaceable> ALTER COLUMN start SET STATISTICS 1000;
ALTER TABLE <replaceable>graph</replaceable>.<replaceable>elabel</replaceable> ALTER COLUMN "end"  SET STATISTICS 1000;
ANALYZE <replaceable>graph</replaceable>.<replaceable>elabel</replaceable>;
</programlisting>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="agensgraph-gql-conformance">
  <title>GQL Conformance</title>

  <indexterm zone="agensgraph-gql-conformance">
   <primary>GQL</primary>
   <secondary>conformance</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   AgensGraph implements part of the ISO GQL standard, ISO/IEC 39075:2024. The tables below say
   which subclauses are covered, which are not, and where AgensGraph goes beyond the standard.
   The subclause numbers are the standard's own. Support is stated as
   <literal>Yes</literal> when the whole subclause is implemented and
   <literal>Partial</literal> when part of it is, with the gap named in the comment.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-gql-supported-features-sect">
   <title>Supported Subclauses</title>
  <para>
   These subclauses are wholly or partly implemented. A <literal>Partial</literal> comment names what is missing, so that a reader porting standard GQL knows what to look for.
  </para>
   <table id="agensgraph-gql-supported-features">
    <title>Supported Subclauses</title>
    <tgroup cols="4">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Subclause</entry>
       <entry>Name</entry>
       <entry>Support</entry>
       <entry>Comment</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry>8.1 to 8.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;start transaction command></literal>, <literal>&lt;transaction characteristics></literal>, <literal>&lt;rollback command></literal>, <literal>&lt;commit command></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>PostgreSQL's own transaction statements answer these. <literal>START TRANSACTION READ ONLY</literal>, <literal>COMMIT</literal> and <literal>ROLLBACK</literal> all work.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>9.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;procedure body></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>NEXT</literal> chains one statement onto the result of the previous one. <literal>NEXT</literal> cannot carry a <literal>YIELD</literal> clause, and the binding variable definition block that 9.2 also allows does not exist, see 10.1 to 10.3 below.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>11.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;graph expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>CURRENT_GRAPH</literal> and <literal>CURRENT_PROPERTY_GRAPH</literal> are accepted, in Cypher and in SQL. They are bare keywords: <literal>current_graph()</literal> is a syntax error. No other form of graph expression exists.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;create graph statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>CREATE GRAPH name</literal> and <literal>CREATE GRAPH IF NOT EXISTS name</literal> work. The graph type the standard requires after the name (<literal>ANY</literal>, <literal>LIKE</literal>, or a graph type reference) is not accepted, nor is <literal>OR REPLACE</literal>, <literal>PROPERTY GRAPH</literal> or <literal>AS COPY OF</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.5</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;drop graph statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>DROP GRAPH name</literal> and <literal>DROP GRAPH IF EXISTS name</literal> work, plus a non standard <literal>CASCADE</literal>, which is needed because a new graph already contains <literal>ag_vertex</literal> and <literal>ag_edge</literal>. <literal>DROP PROPERTY GRAPH</literal> is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>13.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;linear data-modifying statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>A statement composes read clauses and write clauses in one pipeline, and a write clause's changes are visible to a read clause after it.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>13.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;insert statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>INSERT</literal> takes the same pattern as Cypher <literal>CREATE</literal> and is a synonym for it.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>13.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;set statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Property assignment works, including the non standard <literal>+=</literal> merge form. The label set assignment the subclause also defines does not exist: a vertex has exactly one label.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>13.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;remove statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>REMOVE n.prop</literal> removes a property. <literal>REMOVE n:Label</literal> does not exist, for the same reason.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>13.5</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;delete statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>DELETE</literal> and <literal>DETACH DELETE</literal>. Deleting a vertex that still has edges is refused unless <literal>DETACH</literal> is given.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;composite query expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>UNION</literal>, <literal>UNION ALL</literal>, <literal>EXCEPT</literal> and <literal>INTERSECT</literal> compose two query statements. <literal>OTHERWISE</literal> is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;linear query statement></literal> and <literal>&lt;simple query statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>A query is a sequence of the primitive query statements below, ending in a primitive result statement.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;match statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>MATCH</literal> and <literal>OPTIONAL MATCH</literal> work. The braced form, <literal>OPTIONAL { MATCH … MATCH … }</literal>, is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.5</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;call query statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Both call forms work. See 15.2 and 15.3 for what each one requires.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.6</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;filter statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Both <literal>FILTER expr</literal> and <literal>FILTER WHERE expr</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.7</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;let statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Additive, row wise, and able to lead a query. An aggregate in a <literal>LET</literal> expression is refused.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.8</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;for statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Iterating a list works, with <literal>WITH OFFSET AS name</literal> giving a 0-based index. <literal>WITH ORDINALITY</literal> is a syntax error, and <literal>FOR</literal> cannot iterate a binding table.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.9</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;order by and page statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>ORDER BY</literal>, <literal>SKIP</literal>, <literal>OFFSET</literal> and <literal>LIMIT</literal> are statements in their own right and can sit anywhere in a pipeline. Like the standard, AgensGraph does not let one *end* a query, 14.3 requires a primitive result statement last, so a query ending in <literal>ORDER BY</literal> raises <literal>ERROR: Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.10</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;primitive result statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>RETURN</literal>, <literal>RETURN … ORDER BY …</literal>, and <literal>FINISH</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.11</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;return statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>RETURN</literal>, <literal>RETURN DISTINCT</literal>, <literal>RETURN ALL</literal>, <literal>RETURN *</literal> and explicit aliases. <literal>RETURN NO BINDINGS</literal> is a syntax error. Grouping is implicit, see 16.15.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>15.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;call procedure statement></literal> and <literal>&lt;procedure call></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>CALL</literal> and <literal>OPTIONAL CALL</literal>, over either call form.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>15.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;inline procedure call></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>CALL (v) { … }</literal>, <literal>CALL () { … }</literal>, <literal>CALL (*) { … }</literal> and <literal>CALL { WITH v … }</literal> all import correctly. A bare <literal>CALL { … }</literal> deviates: Syntax Rule 3 says an absent variable scope clause is rewritten to one naming *every* field of the incoming working record, so under the standard it imports everything, as <literal>CALL (*) { … }</literal> does. AgensGraph imports nothing instead, and raises no error, so the query answers a different question than it appears to, always write an import list. Write clauses in the subquery body are refused, and <literal>IN TRANSACTIONS</literal> does not exist.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>15.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;named procedure call></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>CALL routine(args) YIELD item [AS alias]</literal> and <literal>YIELD *</literal> work, correlated when an argument references a variable already in scope. <literal>YIELD</literal> is mandatory, where the subclause makes it optional, and the clause can neither lead nor end a query. The name resolves to a PostgreSQL function, not to a procedure of the catalog 17.5 describes: a stock server registers no procedures, and calling one raises <literal>&lt;name>() is a procedure</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;graph pattern binding table></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>A graph pattern produces a binding table. The pattern <literal>YIELD</literal> clause the subclause also allows is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;graph pattern></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Comma-separated path patterns work. The match modes (<literal>DIFFERENT EDGES</literal>, <literal>REPEATABLE ELEMENTS</literal>) and the <literal>KEEP</literal> clause are syntax errors.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.5</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;insert graph pattern></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>The pattern after <literal>INSERT</literal> or <literal>CREATE</literal> may create vertices, edges, or edges between vertices already bound.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.7</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;path pattern expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Concatenation works. Path multiset alternation and path pattern union, two path patterns separated by <literal>\|</literal>, are syntax errors.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.8</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;label expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>A single label name only. Disjunction (<literal>:a\|b</literal>), conjunction (<literal>:a&amp;b</literal>), negation (<literal>:!a</literal>) and the wildcard (<literal>:%</literal>) are all syntax errors. Test a label with the <literal>label()</literal> function in <literal>WHERE</literal> instead.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.9</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;path variable reference></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>MATCH p = (…)</literal> binds the whole path, and <literal>nodes()</literal>, <literal>relationships()</literal> and <literal>length()</literal> read it.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.10</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;element variable reference></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>A pattern variable is in scope for every clause after it.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.11</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;graph pattern quantifier></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>The edge quantifier <literal>-[r*1..3]-></literal> walks a variable number of hops. The standard's quantifier on a parenthesized path pattern, <literal>(…)->{1,3}</literal>, is a syntax error, and so is <literal>+</literal> in place of <literal>*</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.13</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;where clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>WHERE</literal> after a <literal>MATCH</literal>, and inside <literal>FILTER</literal>, <literal>WITH</literal> and a list comprehension. <literal>WHERE</literal> inside a node pattern is a syntax error. A search condition must be a boolean; a property read is not coerced.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.16</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;order by clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Multiple sort keys, <literal>ASC</literal>/<literal>DESC</literal>, <literal>NULLS FIRST</literal>/<literal>NULLS LAST</literal>, expressions as sort keys, and a <literal>RETURN</literal> alias as a sort key.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.17</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;sort specification list></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>As 16.16. An integer sort key is a constant, not an output column number, see Cypher compatibility.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.18</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;limit clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>LIMIT</literal>, including an expression as the count.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.19</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;offset clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Both spellings the subclause defines, <literal>OFFSET</literal> and <literal>SKIP</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;search condition></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>A condition takes a boolean and nothing else, which is what the subclause requires.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>The four predicates below. The nine others 19.6 to 19.13 defines are not supported.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;comparison predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>=</literal>, <literal>&lt;></literal>, <literal>&lt;</literal>, <literal>&lt;=</literal>, <literal>></literal>, <literal>>=</literal>, over scalars, graph elements and lists.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;exists predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Four of the five forms the subclause defines: <literal>EXISTS { pattern }</literal> (with an optional <literal>WHERE</literal>), <literal>EXISTS ( pattern )</literal> (without one, a <literal>WHERE</literal> inside the parentheses is a syntax error), <literal>EXISTS { MATCH … }</literal>, and <literal>EXISTS { &lt;read statement> }</literal>. <literal>EXISTS ( MATCH … )</literal> is a syntax error, and a match statement block of more than one <literal>MATCH</literal> needs a <literal>RETURN</literal> to parse, so Feature GQ22 is not met.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.5</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;null predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>IS NULL</literal> and <literal>IS NOT NULL</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Operators, function calls, <literal>CASE</literal>, subquery expressions and constructors compose freely.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;value expression primary></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Literals, variables, property references, parenthesized expressions.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;value specification></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>SESSION_USER</literal> and <literal>CURRENT_USER</literal>. A parameter is a PostgreSQL parameter, <literal>$1</literal>, supplied by the driver rather than by a GQL general parameter declaration.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.6</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;value query expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>VALUE { … }</literal>, which is all this subclause defines: the body's single value, in its own type, and null when the body is empty. Syntax Rule 10 requires the body to carry <literal>LIMIT 1</literal> or an aggregate; AgensGraph accepts any body and raises <literal>more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression</literal> at run time instead. <literal>EXISTS { }</literal> is 19.4; <literal>COUNT { }</literal>, <literal>COLLECT { }</literal>, <literal>ARRAY { }</literal> and <literal>x IN { }</literal> are extensions, see below.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.7</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;case expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Both the searched and the simple form, plus <literal>NULLIF</literal> and <literal>COALESCE</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.8</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;cast specification></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>The <literal>::</literal> operator casts, and it accepts a keyword type name (<literal>::int</literal>, <literal>::numeric</literal>, <literal>::interval</literal>). <literal>CAST(x AS t)</literal> is a syntax error in Cypher; it works in SQL.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.9</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;aggregate function></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>COUNT(*)</literal>, and the general set functions <literal>AVG</literal>, <literal>COUNT</literal>, <literal>MAX</literal>, <literal>MIN</literal>, <literal>SUM</literal>, <literal>STDDEV_SAMP</literal> and <literal>STDDEV_POP</literal>, each accepting <literal>DISTINCT</literal> or <literal>ALL</literal>. <literal>MIN</literal> and <literal>MAX</literal> over a vertex or edge are ambiguous and must be given a cast or an identity. <literal>COLLECT_LIST</literal> is spelled <literal>collect</literal>. The binary set functions <literal>PERCENTILE_CONT</literal> and <literal>PERCENTILE_DISC</literal> are unreachable from Cypher: both need <literal>WITHIN GROUP</literal>, which is a syntax error there.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.11</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;property reference></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>n.prop</literal>, nested as <literal>n.a.b</literal>, and subscripted as <literal>n.list[0]</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.12</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;binding variable reference></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Names bound by a pattern, <literal>WITH</literal>, <literal>LET</literal>, <literal>FOR</literal> or <literal>YIELD</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.13</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;path value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>A path bound by <literal>MATCH p = (…)</literal> is a first class value of type <literal>graphpath</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.15</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;list value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>[a, b, c]</literal>, subscripting, <literal>IN</literal>, and list comprehensions. <literal>LIST[…]</literal> is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.17</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;list value constructor></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>As 20.15.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.18</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;record constructor></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>{a: 1, b: 2}</literal> builds a map, and <literal>.a</literal> reads a field back. The <literal>RECORD { … }</literal> spelling is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.19</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;field></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Field access on a map, <literal>({a: 1}).a</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.20</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;boolean value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>AND</literal>, <literal>OR</literal>, <literal>NOT</literal>, <literal>XOR</literal>, and <literal>IS [NOT] UNKNOWN</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.21</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;numeric value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>+</literal>, <literal>-</literal>, <literal>*</literal>, <literal>/</literal>, <literal>%</literal>, <literal>^</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.22</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;numeric value function></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry><literal>abs</literal>, <literal>ceil</literal>, <literal>floor</literal>, <literal>round</literal>, <literal>sign</literal>, <literal>sqrt</literal>, <literal>exp</literal>, <literal>ln</literal>, <literal>log</literal>, <literal>log10</literal>, <literal>power</literal>, <literal>mod</literal>, <literal>pi</literal>, the trigonometric functions, <literal>degrees</literal>, <literal>radians</literal>, <literal>char_length</literal>, <literal>octet_length</literal> and <literal>rand</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.23</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;string value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Yes</entry>
       <entry>Concatenation with <literal>\|\|</literal>, and with <literal>+</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.24</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;character string function></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>upper</literal>, <literal>lower</literal>, <literal>btrim</literal>, <literal>ltrim</literal>, <literal>rtrim</literal>, <literal>trim</literal>, <literal>substring(s, from, count)</literal>, <literal>left</literal>, <literal>right</literal>, <literal>replace</literal>, <literal>reverse</literal>, <literal>split</literal>. <literal>NORMALIZE</literal> and the <literal>SUBSTRING(s FROM a FOR b)</literal> spelling are syntax errors in Cypher.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.26</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;datetime value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Date and timestamp arithmetic through PostgreSQL's types.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.27</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;datetime value function></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>CURRENT_DATE</literal>, <literal>CURRENT_TIME</literal>, <literal>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</literal>, <literal>LOCALTIME</literal>, <literal>LOCALTIMESTAMP</literal>. The underscored spellings the standard uses, such as <literal>LOCAL_TIME</literal>, are not recognized.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>21.1</entry>
       <entry>Names and variables</entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>An unquoted identifier folds to lower case; a double quoted identifier keeps its case. The standard's backtick delimited identifier is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>21.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;literal></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry>Integers including hexadecimal, octal, binary and underscore separated forms; floats; strings; <literal>true</literal>, <literal>false</literal> and <literal>null</literal>; lists; maps. The typed literals, <literal>DATE '…'</literal>, <literal>DURATION '…'</literal>, byte strings, are syntax errors, as is <literal>UNKNOWN</literal> used as a value.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>21.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;token></literal>, <literal>&lt;separator></literal>, and <literal>&lt;identifier></literal></entry>
       <entry>Partial</entry>
       <entry><literal>--</literal> and <literal>/* */</literal> delimit comments, as the standard requires. This makes the openCypher anonymous arrows <literal>--></literal> and <literal>--</literal> unusable, see Cypher compatibility.</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-gql-features-not-supported-sect">
   <title>Subclauses Not Supported</title>
  <para>
   None of the syntax these subclauses define is accepted. The comment gives the AgensGraph route to the same result where there is one.
  </para>
   <table id="agensgraph-gql-features-not-supported">
    <title>Subclauses Not Supported</title>
    <tgroup cols="4">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Subclause</entry>
       <entry>Name</entry>
       <entry>Support</entry>
       <entry>Comment</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry>7.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;session set command></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>SESSION SET</literal> is a syntax error. Set the working graph with <literal>SET graph_path = name</literal>, which is a PostgreSQL setting and follows PostgreSQL's session and transaction scoping.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>7.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;session reset command></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Use <literal>RESET graph_path</literal> or <literal>RESET ALL</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>7.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;session close command></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Close the connection.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>7.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;session parameter specification></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>10.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;graph variable definition></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None. A query addresses one graph, named by <literal>graph_path</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>10.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;binding table variable definition></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>A <literal>WITH</literal> or <literal>LET</literal> binding, or a SQL common table expression around the Cypher, carries an intermediate result forward.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>10.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;value variable definition></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Use <literal>LET</literal>, which is 14.7 and is supported.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>11.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;binding table expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>11.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;object expression primary></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;linear catalog-modifying statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Catalog changes are PostgreSQL DDL statements and are not composable into a query pipeline.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;create schema statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>A graph *is* a schema; <literal>CREATE GRAPH</literal> creates it. SQL <literal>CREATE SCHEMA</literal> also exists but does not make a graph.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.3</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;drop schema statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>DROP GRAPH</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.6</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;create graph type statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None. A label is untyped, except for a property promoted to a column, which acquires that column's type. See <literal>CREATE VLABEL</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.7</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;drop graph type statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>12.8</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;call catalog-modifying procedure statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>13.6</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;call data-modifying procedure statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>A write clause inside a <literal>CALL</literal> body is refused.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;composite query statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>The optional <literal>USE</literal> prefix a composite query statement carries has no counterpart; the composite query expression itself, 14.2, is supported.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>14.12</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;select statement></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>SELECT … FROM MATCH (…)</literal> is a syntax error. SQL <literal>SELECT</literal> over a Cypher subquery does the same job: see SQL and Cypher.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.1</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;at schema clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>A statement runs in the database it is connected to.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.2</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;use graph clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>SET graph_path = name</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.6</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;path pattern prefix></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Neither the path modes (<literal>WALK</literal>, <literal>TRAIL</literal>, <literal>SIMPLE</literal>, <literal>ACYCLIC</literal>) nor the path search prefixes (<literal>ALL</literal>, <literal>ANY</literal>, <literal>ANY SHORTEST</literal>, <literal>ALL SHORTEST</literal>, <literal>SHORTEST k</literal>, <literal>SHORTEST k GROUPS</literal>) parse. <literal>shortestpath()</literal>, <literal>allshortestpaths()</literal> and <literal>dijkstra()</literal> cover shortest path search.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.12</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;simplified path pattern expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>The <literal>-/ … /-></literal> form is a syntax error.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>16.15</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;group by clause></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>An explicit <literal>GROUP BY</literal> is a syntax error in Cypher. Grouping is implicit: the non aggregated items of a <literal>RETURN</literal> or <literal>WITH</literal> are the grouping keys. SQL <literal>GROUP BY</literal> is available in SQL.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>17.1 to 17.8</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;schema reference></literal> and the other object references</entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>An object is named by a PostgreSQL schema qualified name. The standard's directory paths, <literal>/dir/schema/graph</literal>, do not parse.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>18.1 to 18.10</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;nested graph type specification></literal> and the other type elements</entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.6</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;value type predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>IS TYPED</literal> and <literal>IS ::</literal> are syntax errors. Cast and compare, or use <literal>pg_typeof</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.7</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;normalized predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>None.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.8</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;directed predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Every edge is directed.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.9</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;labeled predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>IS LABELED</literal> is a syntax error, and so is <literal>n:Label</literal> in an expression. Write <literal>label(n) = 'person'</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.10</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;source/destination predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>IS SOURCE OF</literal> and <literal>IS DESTINATION OF</literal> are syntax errors. Compare identities: <literal>id(a) = id(startNode(r))</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.11</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;all_different predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Compare identities pairwise.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.12</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;same predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>id(a) = id(b)</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>19.13</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;property_exists predicate></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>PROPERTY_EXISTS(n, key)</literal> is a syntax error. Write <literal>exists(n.key)</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;dynamic parameter specification></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>The driver's parameter placeholder, <literal>$1</literal>, takes its place.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.5</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;let value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>LET … IN</literal> inside an expression is a syntax error. The <literal>LET</literal> statement, 14.7, is supported.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.10</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;element_id function></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>element_id()</literal> does not exist. <literal>id()</literal> returns the identity, as a <literal>graphid</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.14</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;path value constructor></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>A path comes from a pattern, not from a constructor.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.16</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;list value function></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>trim(list, n)</literal> and <literal>elements()</literal> do not exist. <literal>head</literal>, <literal>last</literal>, <literal>tail</literal>, <literal>size</literal> and <literal>cardinality</literal> do.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.25</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;byte string function></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>PostgreSQL's <literal>bytea</literal> functions are reachable from SQL.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.28</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;duration value expression></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>PostgreSQL's <literal>interval</literal> type covers durations, but only from SQL: <literal>INTERVAL '1 day'</literal> is a syntax error in Cypher.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>20.29</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;duration value function></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry><literal>duration()</literal> does not exist.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>21.4</entry>
       <entry><literal>&lt;GQL terminal character></literal></entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>The lexer is PostgreSQL's, extended for Cypher; it does not implement the standard's character set rules.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>22.1 to 22.20</entry>
       <entry>Additional common rules</entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>These subclauses define the standard's evaluation machinery rather than syntax. AgensGraph evaluates a query through the PostgreSQL planner and executor, so they are not implemented as written.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>23.1</entry>
       <entry>GQLSTATUS</entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>An error carries a SQLSTATE. See Error codes.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>23.2</entry>
       <entry>Diagnostic records</entry>
       <entry>No</entry>
       <entry>Diagnostics come from PostgreSQL's error report fields.</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="agensgraph-gql-extensions-beyond-the-standard-sect">
   <title>Extensions Beyond the Standard</title>
  <para>
   AgensGraph accepts a good deal that ISO/IEC 39075 does not define. Some of it is openCypher, which the product supported first. Some of it exists because a graph here is stored in PostgreSQL and both halves are addressable.
  </para>
   <table id="agensgraph-gql-extensions-beyond-the-standard">
    <title>Extensions Beyond the Standard</title>
    <tgroup cols="2">
     <thead>
      <row>
       <entry>Extension</entry>
       <entry>What it adds</entry>
      </row>
     </thead>
     <tbody>
      <row>
       <entry>A SQL statement over a Cypher subquery, and Cypher over a label table</entry>
       <entry>A Cypher query is a table expression and a label is a table, so one statement can join graph and relational data. See SQL and Cypher.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>LOAD FROM table AS r CREATE (…)</literal></entry>
       <entry>Creates graph elements from the rows of a relation, including a foreign table. See Loading data.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Label inheritance</entry>
       <entry><literal>CREATE VLABEL child INHERITS (parent)</literal> makes a hierarchy of labels, and a match on the parent also returns the children. <literal>MATCH (n:parent ONLY)</literal> restricts the match to the parent. See Label inheritance.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>SET n += map</literal></entry>
       <entry>Merges a map into the properties instead of replacing them.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>dijkstra()</literal>, <literal>shortestpath()</literal>, <literal>allshortestpaths()</literal></entry>
       <entry>Weighted and unweighted shortest path search, in place of the standard's path search prefixes.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Promoted properties</entry>
       <entry><literal>ALTER VLABEL movie ADD COLUMN year int GENERATED</literal> gives a property a typed, indexable column, and a property read resolves to it. See <literal>ALTER VLABEL</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>Graph DDL</entry>
       <entry><literal>CREATE VLABEL</literal>, <literal>CREATE ELABEL</literal>, <literal>CREATE PROPERTY INDEX</literal>, <literal>CREATE CONSTRAINT</literal>, <literal>REINDEX VLABEL</literal> and <literal>ALTER VLABEL</literal>. See Data definition.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>openCypher clauses</entry>
       <entry><literal>UNWIND</literal>, <literal>MERGE</literal> with <literal>ON CREATE</literal> and <literal>ON MATCH</literal>, and <literal>WITH</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry><literal>COUNT { }</literal>, <literal>COLLECT { }</literal>, <literal>ARRAY { }</literal>, <literal>x IN { }</literal></entry>
       <entry>Four subquery value expressions the standard has no form for, 20.9 defines only <literal>COUNT(*)</literal>, <literal>COUNT(expr)</literal> and the <literal>COLLECT_LIST</literal> aggregate over rows. <literal>COUNT { }</literal> and <literal>COLLECT { }</literal> come from openCypher, <literal>ARRAY { }</literal> from SQL's <literal>ARRAY(subquery)</literal>, and <literal>x IN { }</literal> from SQL's <literal>IN (subquery)</literal>. See Subquery value expressions.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>openCypher operators and functions</entry>
       <entry><literal>STARTS WITH</literal>, <literal>ENDS WITH</literal>, <literal>CONTAINS</literal>, <literal>=~</literal>, list comprehensions, <literal>all</literal>/<literal>any</literal>/<literal>none</literal>/<literal>single</literal>, <literal>keys</literal>, <literal>properties</literal>, <literal>id</literal>, <literal>label</literal>, <literal>labels</literal>, <literal>type</literal>, <literal>startNode</literal>, <literal>endNode</literal>, <literal>nodes</literal>, <literal>relationships</literal>, <literal>length</literal>, <literal>range</literal>, <literal>head</literal>, <literal>last</literal>, <literal>tail</literal>, <literal>toString</literal>, <literal>toInteger</literal>, <literal>toFloat</literal>.</entry>
      </row>
      <row>
       <entry>PostgreSQL's whole surface</entry>
       <entry>Every SQL type, function, index method, extension and setting is available to a graph query, which is how vector and full text search work. See vector search.</entry>
      </row>
     </tbody>
    </tgroup>
   </table>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>

</appendix>
